---
import { Navbar } from "../../components/Navbar";
import { ScrollUpButton } from "../../components/ScrollUpButton";
import { ListItem } from "../../components/blog/ListItem";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.vue";
---

<Layout
  title="How tunc creates live ground truth on a real map"
  ogImage="https://tunc.app/tunc.png"
  description="Learn how tunc turns fresh Android camera captures into location-bound, hardware-signed ground truth that people can explore on the tunc.live map."
  keywords="tunc, tunc app, tunc live, live map, real-time photo map, ground truth, verified photos, hardware signed photos, OSINT map, live Android camera app, location-bound photos"
  author="tunc"
  publishedDate="2026-07-31"
  modifiedDate="2026-07-31"
  articleSchema={true}
>
  <Navbar client:load />
  <div class="flex justify-center bg-bgDark2 relative pt-2">
    <div class="px-2 sm:px-4">
      <article
        class="p-8 text-center rounded-3xl w-full lg:w-[1200px] 2xl:w-[1400px] mb-24 mt-16 sm:mt-24"
      >
        <header class="mb-12">
          <div class="text-sm text-secondaryText my-4">31 July 2026</div>
          <h1
            class="text-5xl sm:text-6xl lg:text-7xl xl:text-8xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-none mb-6 bg-gradient-to-r from-white via-orange-200 to-emerald-300 bg-clip-text text-transparent"
          >
            How tunc creates live ground truth on a real map
          </h1>
          <p class="text-lg text-secondaryText mb-4">
            Tunc is an Android camera app for fresh, location-bound captures.
            <a
              href="https://tunc.live"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="text-teal-400 font-bold">tunc.live</a
            > is where published captures become points people can explore on a real map.
          </p>
        </header>

        <img
          src="https://tunc.app/tunc.png"
          alt="tunc logo - ground truth at this exact moment"
          class="rounded-xl mb-8 w-40 sm:w-52 mx-auto mt-8"
          width="512"
          height="512"
          loading="eager"
        />

        <section
          class="text-secondaryText !leading-8 sm:!leading-10 sm:text-xl text-left sm:text-justify mx-auto w-full md:w-10/12 lg:w-2/3 mt-16"
        >
          <a
            href="https://tunc.app"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-teal-400 font-bold">tunc</a
          > is built around a simple question: what is happening at this exact moment?
          Most posts reach the internet after an event has already changed. They may be
          delayed, cropped, edited, reposted, or separated from the place and time they
          claim to show. tunc is designed for a more immediate form of evidence: a fresh
          camera capture with location and time bound to it when it is made.

          <h2 class="text-white text-3xl my-4">Why tunc exists</h2>

          Social feeds are optimized for attention, not context. When an event is moving
          quickly, one image rarely tells the whole story. People need nearby points of
          view: the crowd at an entrance, the condition of a road, activity near a stage,
          or the quieter street a few blocks away.

          <br />
          <br />
          tunc separates the act of capturing from the act of exploring. The Android app
          creates a new capture in the moment. The public map at
          <a
            href="https://tunc.live"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-teal-400 font-bold"> tunc.live</a
          > places approved captures in their geographic context, so a viewer can move
          from a broad map view to an individual point of view.

          <h2 class="text-white text-3xl my-4">How the tunc app works</h2>

          tunc removes the usual account and gallery workflow. Rather than uploading an
          existing image, the app is made to capture directly from the camera, then bind
          its provenance to the submitted data.

          <ListItem
            list={[
              {
                title: "Claim a name without a password",
                description:
                  "A user chooses a unique username. The Android device generates a cryptographic key pair in its hardware-backed security area, while the private key stays on that device.",
              },
              {
                title: "Wait for an accurate location",
                description:
                  "The capture flow waits for an accurate GPS lock before the shutter is available, helping associate the resulting capture with meaningful coordinates.",
              },
              {
                title: "Create a fresh camera capture",
                description:
                  "The app captures from the camera rather than accepting an image from the phone gallery. Capture time and location become part of the submission context.",
              },
              {
                title: "Sign and submit the capture",
                description:
                  "The device signs the capture payload using its device-bound key. The backend can check that signature against the corresponding public key.",
              },
              {
                title: "Review before public publication",
                description:
                  "Submitted captures are reviewed before they can be published to the public map. This creates a deliberate boundary between submitting a capture and making it visible to everyone.",
              },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />

          <h2 class="text-white text-3xl my-4">How captures appear on tunc.live</h2>

          <a
            href="https://tunc.live"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-teal-400 font-bold">tunc.live</a
          > is a separate public website for viewing published captures. It loads recent
          public capture data and plots each valid latitude and longitude as a marker on a
          dark, interactive map. When capture points are available, the map frames the
          visible area around them.

          <br />
          <br />
          Select a marker to open the capture detail view. The viewer can inspect the
          image along with its coordinates and capture time, then return to the map to
          compare it with other nearby perspectives. This is what makes the system useful
          for live events, local awareness, and open-source research: the map provides
          context that a standalone image cannot.

          <h2 class="text-white text-3xl my-4">What people can do with the live map</h2>

          <ListItem
            list={[
              {
                title: "Scan for activity",
                description:
                  "Start with the map to see where approved capture points are appearing and identify areas worth examining.",
              },
              {
                title: "Open the point of view behind a marker",
                description:
                  "Choose a marker to view its capture and the associated geographic and time context.",
              },
              {
                title: "Compare nearby perspectives",
                description:
                  "Several captures in the same area can give a clearer picture of an event than a single isolated post.",
              },
              {
                title: "Follow evidence, not an algorithm",
                description:
                  "The map is organized around place and published captures, helping viewers inspect the source material directly.",
              },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />

          <h2 class="text-white text-3xl my-4">One system, two ways to participate</h2>

          The tunc Android app is where a live capture starts. The
          <a
            href="https://tunc.live"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-teal-400 font-bold"> tunc.live</a
          > map is where approved captures can be understood in relation to place, time, and
          other points of view. Together, they aim to make real-time information more
          inspectable: not a claim detached from context, but a capture that can be seen
          on a real map.

          <h4 class="text-white text-xl my-4">Explore tunc</h4>

          Learn more about the Android app and download it at
          <a
            href="https://tunc.app"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-teal-400 font-bold"> tunc.app</a
          >. To see published captures on the map, visit
          <a
            href="https://tunc.live"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-teal-400 font-bold"> tunc.live</a
          >.
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
  <ScrollUpButton client:load />
  <Footer client:only="vue" />
</Layout>